.TH REFFECTOR 1 "2026-10-16" "reffector" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
reffector \- investigate Go effects by source site and reverse callers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B reffector
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ packages ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Analyzes Go source without executing functions, initializers or tests, modifying
analyzed source, or performing refactoring. The default view is effect category,
then distinct direct source operation site, then reverse caller relationships.
Results describe possible static reachability, not proof of execution.
.PP
Local directory arguments recurse, including the default current directory.
Use -exact to select just each named local package. Explicit Go patterns such
as ./... and import paths are preserved. The current Go build environment
selects files. Dependencies are inspected for propagation; operation origins
can live in dependencies reached from selected source functions.
.SH INVESTIGATION
Start with effect groups at -max-depth 0. Select independent operation sites,
then expand reverse callers. Inspect controlling source, policy, authorization,
failure handling and cleanup. Compare independent paths, not merely shared
category names. Propose simplification only when justified: identify the old
independent responsibilities or bypasses that would disappear, where effects
remain, and tests that verify behavior. Never consolidate automatically because
two sites share a category. Completing analysis is not completing a review.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -audit-unresolved
Write uncertain source sites as JSON Lines, without reverse graphs or site
limits. Default excludes sites with established effects, but retains VTA-only
possible effects. Uses the same analysis, not another engine. Stdout is records
only; -stats writes coverage to stderr. SSA building remains in memory.
Only -audit-include-known, -all, -tests, -exact and -stats may accompany this
mode. Explicit filters, view/JSON flags and traversal limits are errors.
.TP
.B -audit-include-known
With -audit-unresolved, include recognized-effect sites that also have
uncertainty. Without audit mode this flag is an error.
.PP
Audit site records have record_type site and schema_version 1, the original
canonical site ID, owner/location, API alternatives, operation evidence and all
reasons. primary_reason is the first lexicographically sorted reason (unknown
if absent), not severity. unknown_api explicitly marks unnamed alternatives.
origin/selected, dispatch_kinds, ssa_nodes and vta_candidates preserve existing
SSA identities and variants. Candidate counts include raw VTA alternatives;
effect_propagation_supported identifies those accepted for propagation.
possible_effects derives only from accepted candidates at this call instruction,
not unrelated enclosing-function effects. Transitive candidate effects are not
proof of execution. Declaration boundaries have kind declaration, not call;
summary-boundary site evidence may be uncertainty-only. Invalid-position and
synthetic/non-source origins are excluded as in the ordinary site universe.
.PP
In audit mode -all appends record_type function records for the full selected
source inventory, with owner locations. Select controls using empty effects,
empty possible_effects and false uncertain together, not the legacy coverage
category alone. No such selection proves purity. Encoding is one record at a
time; output-write failures can leave partial JSONL. See README for schema details.
.TP
.B -view effects|functions
Default effects: readable groups, canonical sites and bounded reverse graph.
Functions: compatible function/effect text pairs or function-record JSON array.
.TP
.B -json
Write structured JSON for the selected view. Deliberate compatibility change:
-json alone is now an effect-report object, not a function array. Use
-view functions -json for the unchanged function-record schema.
.TP
.B -details
Compatibility function/witness JSON object (records, stats, details). Implies
function view and JSON; conflicts with explicit -view effects. Retains shortest
witnesses rather than the full bounded reverse subgraph.
.TP
.B -exact
Do not recursively expand local directory arguments.
.TP
.B -tests
Include internal/external test-file functions, never execute tests. Generated
test executables are excluded; source variants merge only in summaries
and inventory.
.TP
.B -all
Add full unfiltered selected source inventory in effect view, even under
filters.
In function view, include empty summaries (summary filters still apply).
Synthetic init, wrappers, lowered iterator helpers and test executables are not
source inventory; source closures and explicit init declarations are included.
.TP
.B -stats
Also write coverage JSON to stderr. Effect reports always include coverage.
.TP
.B -direct
In effect view, restrict origins to selected roots rather than reachable helpers,
including VTA candidates. Explicit unresolved leads remain available.
In function view, report only recognized operations in the function itself,
never inherited or VTA-only effects; uncertainty still propagates.
Unfiltered inventory and contract-implementation summaries are unchanged.
.TP
.B -package substring, -function substring
Select source roots by case-sensitive substring, retaining their reachable
helper origins. Prefix a function name with = for an exact local function
identity, e.g. -function '=(*App).Start'. Unlike substring matching, this does
not select uncalled source closures as independent roots. Reverse callers
stay within this reachable subgraph; remove root filters to see other callers.
Function-view filters select summary/witness roots.
.TP
.B -effect substring
Select effect categories by case-sensitive substring. Function view matches and
filters both effects and possible_effects without changing their evidence labels.
Does not rewrite site metadata or the full effect-view inventory.
.TP
.B -api identity
Select an exact package/receiver-qualified API, such as os.ReadFile or
net/http.(ResponseWriter).WriteHeader. Effect view only.
.TP
.B -operation substring
Explicitly substring-based API search. Effect view or compatibility -details;
does not filter function-summary records.
.TP
.B -site ID
Select one canonical direct site ID copied from output. Effect view only.
.TP
.B -contracts
Include method-set-compatible implementation leads for matching interface call
sites. Leads are labelled established, candidate_vta, or contract_compatible;
the latter is not a call edge. Implementation effects use established calls;
possible_effects require VTA edges (shown as possible= in text). Lists are sorted
and possible-only effects exclude established ones across aggregated variants.
One source site's contract combines the strongest relationship and effect
evidence across generic/test variants without merging graph nodes or creating
execution edges. Any open variant keeps the contract open. Effect view only.
.TP
.B -dependency-vta-functions N
Opt into VTA expansion through at most N loaded dependency functions. Requires a
positive -dependency-vta-edges budget. Candidate relationships remain
candidate_vta, never established calls.
.TP
.B -dependency-vta-edges N
Bound static dependency call edges traversed for VTA expansion. Requires a
positive -dependency-vta-functions budget. Proven concrete interface inputs
may exclude incompatible candidate effects for a selected root; open and
unresolved inputs retain candidates. A per-root context-state budget equal to
the sum of both VTA budgets bounds this refinement. On context truncation the
analysis falls back globally to conservative VTA propagation. Coverage reports
context_states, context_budget, context_truncated and context_roots_fallback
(selected SSA root contexts, including variants). Dependency function/edge
truncation is reported separately. Without these flags, default analysis
behavior is unchanged.
.TP
.B -unresolved
Switch from the default recognized operation sites to unresolved leads without
recognized effects, not confirmed operation sites. Effect view only. Semantic
summaries with known effects and open dispatch
remain in their effect categories, not this unresolved-only selection.
.TP
.B -reason substring
Select uncertainty reasons by case-sensitive substring in the effect view.
Works on recognized operations or, with -unresolved, effect-free leads.
For example, -unresolved -reason interface_dispatch excludes opaque stdlib
logging calls, but does not classify remaining calls as consequential.
Combine with -api or -operation and inspect the cited interface contract.
Site metadata and coverage remain unchanged. Keep -unresolved when revisiting
an unresolved-only site with -site ID.
.TP
.B -max-depth N
Maximum reverse source-call depth, default 6. Zero shows origins only; one adds
immediate callers. At positive depths synthetic SSA forwarding costs zero source
edges. All supported edges among discovered nodes are retained within limits,
including cycle edges between boundary nodes. In -details, bounds witness
length.
.TP
.B -max-sites N
Maximum emitted source sites, default 1000, positive.
.TP
.B -max-edges N
Maximum unique emitted reverse edges across sites, default 1000, positive.
Shared edges consume the budget once. Effect view only.
.TP
.B -max-paths N
Compatibility -details witness budget, default 1000, positive. Not a
reverse-graph
path enumeration option.
.TP
.B -h, -help
Print usage and exit successfully.
.SH EFFECT OUTPUT
Human output defines sites once and references them from effect groups. Each
site shows source symbol, exact APIs, file/line/column, recognition, effects and
uncertainty reasons. Local n1-style labels refer to shared nodes;
caller -> callee
lines cite exact source calls. Shared/revisited nodes and cycles are explicit.
Use canonical site IDs, not local node labels, for later queries.
.PP
Effect JSON has schema_version 1:
.TP
.B groups
Effect identifiers and canonical site ID references. A multi-category operation
uses the same site ID in every group; independent calls use independent IDs.
.TP
.B sites, unresolved_sites
Canonical source-site definitions and unresolved-only ID references. Site-wide
APIs/effects/reasons are unions. The operations array preserves each API
alternative's own effects, recognition and uncertainty. Kind is call, go or
defer for those source forms, or declaration for missing-body/opaque-boundary
origins. Locations identify SSA operations/calls or declarations. IDs use package,
source function and location, not checkout directory, filters or traversal
order;
they are not stable across source-location changes.
.TP
.B reverse
Per-site origin node IDs, node ID/depth references, edge ID references, revisit,
cycle and truncated flags. This is a bounded subgraph, not one shortest path.
.TP
.B nodes, edges
Shared variant-specific SSA-node definitions and edges. Nodes carry source
symbols, variants, selected/dependency status and direct/inherited uncertainty.
Edges reference caller/callee node IDs and source call evidence. Source edges
have kind=call, go or defer, preserving ordinary, goroutine-launch and deferred
invocation forms. This additive field leaves edge IDs and schema_version
unchanged. An established go edge identifies a possible spawned body, not
completion; defer identifies work registered for a later function exit, not
immediate execution. Source resolution is established or candidate_vta;
candidates are possible VTA flow, not execution. Synthetic forwarding has
synthetic=true and no fabricated source location. Never join nodes by
source-symbol names: that can invent paths across incompatible generic
instantiations or test variants.
.TP
.B coverage, inventory
Coverage plus optional -all unfiltered source-function inventory and opt-in
interface_contracts. Contract-compatible implementations are investigation
leads, not execution edges.
Emitted/omitted
count the compatible summary projection, not graph size. Other counts retain
selected packages/source functions across filters: effectful, direct, inherited,
uncertain, unresolved_only, no_effects_no_uncertainty.
Direct/inherited/uncertain
can overlap. Site counts distinguish identified operations (selected versus
dependency), unresolved-only leads, filter matches and emitted sites. Empty or
no-effects/no-uncertainty results never prove purity.
.TP
.B truncated
Report-level sites/edges/depth and per-reverse-graph edges/depth flags identify
omissions. Cycle/revisit flags do not mean relationships were omitted. Limits
bound output explanations, not Go loading/SSA cost. Reverse limits are in
reverse[].truncated.
.SH FUNCTION COMPATIBILITY
-view functions text contains package.Function effect pairs, with receiver
identities for methods and $1-style closure suffixes. Only recognized effects
appear. JSON is an array of objects with package, function, effects,
uncertain and optional possible_effects. Each effects item has identifier and
direct; possible_effects lists VTA-only effects, not established reachability.
Effects may be empty.
Uncertain means unresolved or opaque behavior is reachable; it is not an effect.
.PP
-details emits records, stats and details. Sites group one deterministic
shortest
forward witness per selected root/site. Witness edge IDs reference
details.call_edges; direct means zero edges. details.uncertainty distinguishes
direct and inherited uncertainty (both may be true). Its depth/paths/sites
truncation flags retain their previous meanings. Use the default reverse graph
when alternate callers or multiple call sites matter. All projections use the
same analysis, not separate resolution frameworks.
.SH UNCERTAINTY
Direct grouped origins retain reasons: interface_dispatch (unresolved interface
target, including a sync.Locker method), function_value (unsupported
parameter/memory/result flow), missing_body,
unrecognized_stdlib, reflection_opaque, callback (summary hooks/drivers),
argument_condition (a summarized effect depends on unknown argument values),
state_condition (a summarized effect depends on prior state), and
unsupported_resolution (including builtin printing). Inherited uncertainty
is traceable through graph edges or forward witnesses, not duplicated origins.
Effect/API filters can hide origins; remove them or query -unresolved as
appropriate.
.PP
Named ResponseWriter.Write/WriteHeader contracts report possible http.response
while open dispatch remains uncertain, without a concrete target edge.
A response
may be recorded in memory. Unknown io.Writer.Write remains unclassified.
.SH EFFECT VOCABULARY
fs.read, fs.write, fs.create, fs.remove, fs.rename;
net.connect, net.listen, net.close; http.response;
resource.acquire, resource.release; process.exec, process.exit;
env.read, env.write; db.query, db.exec; process.control;
process.signal.register, process.signal.unregister, process.signal.ignore,
process.signal.reset; context.cancel;
concurrency.goroutine (go statement, WaitGroup.Go, or possible
NotifyContext watcher);
channel.send, channel.receive, channel.select, channel.close;
concurrency.lock, concurrency.unlock; concurrency.task, concurrency.wait
(sync.WaitGroup, sync.Once.Do, sync.Cond.Wait, and invocations of returned
functions from sync.OnceFunc, sync.OnceValue, and sync.OnceValues);
concurrency.condition.signal and concurrency.condition.broadcast (exact
sync.Cond.Signal/Broadcast); concurrency.atomic (exact sync/atomic APIs);
concurrency.map.read and concurrency.map.write
(exact sync.Map operations); concurrency.pool.get and concurrency.pool.put
(exact sync.Pool operations); crypto.x509.certpool.add (attempted additions
through exact crypto/x509.CertPool APIs); crypto.x509.certpool.system (exact
crypto/x509.SystemCertPool calls);
concurrency.callback.register (context.AfterFunc, time.AfterFunc),
concurrency.callback.stop (context.AfterFunc), concurrency.timer.schedule
(time.After, NewTimer, NewTicker, Tick), concurrency.timer.stop (exact Timer and
Ticker methods), concurrency.timer.reset (time.Timer and Ticker), and
concurrency.sleep (time.Sleep when the duration may be positive).
.PP
Summaries use resolved Go package/receiver identities, never method names alone.
os.Process.Kill/Signal report process.control; os.Process.Wait/Release report
resource.release, without asserting successful termination or completion (Wait
may block). Exact os/signal.Notify calls can report process.signal.register for
channel registration only; they do not establish signal delivery or receiver
execution. A statically nil channel is excluded because Notify panics before
registering, as are statically known custom signal types unsupported by the
platform implementation. Unknown channel or signal values retain the effect as
uncertain with argument_condition. Even os.Interrupt and syscall.Signal values
remain uncertain: the summary does not prove runtime value or platform support,
and os.Interrupt is a mutable package variable. With no signal arguments, Notify
requests all signals. NotifyContext can report possible process.signal.register
and concurrency.goroutine effects with argument_condition uncertainty: the
parent may be nil, explicit signal support can be platform/value dependent, and
the watcher is skipped when the returned context is already done. It does not
establish signal delivery or cancellation by an arriving signal. Exact
os/signal.Stop calls report possible process.signal.unregister with
state_condition uncertainty: the call does not establish that the channel was
previously registered. Stop(nil) is excluded as a no-op, and the analyzer does
not track registrations across calls.
.PP
A stop function is recognized only when local SSA directly traces its
context.CancelFunc value to the second result of
os/signal.NotifyContext. Calling it reports context.cancel and
process.signal.unregister: it cancels the returned
context and stops relaying for its internal channel, releasing associated
resources. There may be nothing to unregister if no supported signal was
registered; the report does not prove a signal was registered or delivered.
Arbitrary, mixed and helper-returned function values remain unresolved.
Exact Ignore/Reset calls can report distinct signal-policy effects.
With no signal arguments, including a nil or proven empty slice passed with ...,
each applies to all signals; a single nil os.Signal argument is unsupported.
Signal-list inspection requires a single-use local array slice with proven
bounds and initialization. Helper uses, escaping aliases and dynamic bounds
retain argument_condition uncertainty instead of assuming initial values survive.
Statically unsupported concrete signal types in proven lists are excluded, while
syscall signal values and unknown argument flows retain argument_condition
uncertainty. These effects do not establish signal delivery, correlate prior
registrations, or prove a runtime signal state change.
.PP
context.cancel is also reported for CancelFunc values locally traced to
WithCancel, WithDeadline, WithDeadlineCause, WithTimeout or WithTimeoutCause,
or CancelCauseFunc values traced to WithCancelCause. Other function values
remain unresolved because these named types can wrap arbitrary code.
.PP
Exact context.AfterFunc calls report concurrency.callback.register with
callback uncertainty: they arrange asynchronous callback work after context
cancellation, but the callback may never run and its body is not followed as a
synchronous call. Exact time.AfterFunc calls report the same effect with
callback uncertainty: they schedule the supplied callback asynchronously when
the duration elapses; callback execution and completion are not inferred.
Exact time.After and time.NewTimer calls report concurrency.timer.schedule
for one-shot notifications. Exact time.NewTicker and time.Tick calls report the
same effect for recurring notifications. Unknown ticker intervals retain
argument_condition uncertainty; a statically non-positive NewTicker interval
has no scheduling effect because it panics, while time.Tick returns nil for a
statically non-positive interval. Exact time.Ticker.Stop reports
concurrency.timer.stop: the API prevents future ticks after the call completes,
but does not close the channel. The report identifies the call, not proof the
receiver was non-nil or the call completed.
Exact time.Timer.Stop and Reset calls report concurrency.timer.stop and
concurrency.timer.reset with state_condition uncertainty. These identify exact
method attempts, not whether a timer was active, whether it stopped firing, or
whether it was rescheduled. Exact time.Ticker.Reset reports
concurrency.timer.reset for a known positive period. Unknown periods retain
argument_condition; a statically non-positive period is unresolved because
Reset panics. Reset on an AfterFunc timer can schedule another callback
without waiting for a prior callback to complete. Neither method is
treated as waiting for callback completion, and same-named application methods
do not match. Exact time.Sleep calls with a statically positive duration
report concurrency.sleep; a statically non-positive duration has no sleep
effect, while an unknown duration retains the possible effect with
argument_condition because Sleep returns immediately for non-positive values.
This identifies a possible delay of the current goroutine, not its exact
duration or runtime scheduling. A returned context.AfterFunc stop function
is recognized only
when local SSA traces every value path directly to context.AfterFunc. Calling
it reports
concurrency.callback.stop with state_condition uncertainty: it stops the
callback only if it has not started; a false result may mean it already started
or was stopped before. It does not wait for completion or cancel the context.
Arbitrary, mixed and helper-returned stop functions remain unresolved; methods
with the same name are not matched.
Calls into summarized standard-library APIs and recognized SSA operations are
direct operations; effects reached through application helpers are inherited.
SSA concurrency categories identify goroutine launches and channel coordination;
they do not prove completion, blocking or deadlock. Exact built-in close calls
report channel.close as an operation attempt, not a successful state transition;
closing a nil or already closed channel panics, and receiver completion is not
inferred. go/defer sites identify
launch/registration forms, not synchronous execution. sync lock categories name
recognized Mutex/RWMutex operations, not ownership or deadlock. Exact
sync.WaitGroup.Add/Done/Go calls report concurrency.task; Wait reports
concurrency.wait. Add adjusts the counter; Add or Done can panic if it becomes
negative. Positive additions to an empty group must happen before Wait. Done
decrements the counter. These describe task-counter operations, not correct
accounting or guaranteed work completion. WaitGroup.Go also reports a goroutine
launch; its callback must not panic and retains callback uncertainty. Exact
sync.Once.Do calls also report concurrency.wait: calls return only after the
once callback finishes, and concurrent calls may wait for it. The callback
remains uncertain; callback effects are not inherited without a proven call
edge. Invocation of a returned function is also recognized when local SSA proves
every value path comes from the same exact sync.OnceFunc, sync.OnceValue, or
sync.OnceValues constructor. This reports the wait at invocation, not wrapper
construction. Arbitrary, mixed, helper-returned, and memory-loaded function
values remain unresolved. Callback effects are not inherited without a proven
call edge. These summaries do not establish callback execution per call or task
completion. Same-named methods on other types do not match.
.PP
Exact sync.Cond.Wait reports concurrency.wait, concurrency.unlock, and
concurrency.lock: it releases the associated sync.Locker while waiting and
reacquires it before returning. Because the locker is an interface, its concrete
method dispatch remains unresolved and is retained as interface_dispatch
uncertainty. The report does not identify a concrete lock or inherit effects
from its methods.
Callers should recheck their condition in a loop; a wake does not establish that
an application predicate is true. Exact sync.Cond.Signal and Broadcast report
distinct one-waiter and all-waiter notification attempts with state_condition
uncertainty because the current waiter set is unknown. These effects do not
establish which goroutine is awakened or that it runs promptly; the analysis
does not pair a notification with the Wait calls it unblocks or report their
conditional synchronizes-before relation. Same-named methods and open interfaces
are not treated as exact Cond operations.
.PP
Exact sync/atomic functions and typed methods report concurrency.atomic. Go defines atomic
operations as sequentially consistent, with a synchronizes-before relation when
one operation observes another. A site does not establish shared state, which
operations observe each other, protocol correctness or successful completion.
Exact sync.Map Load and Range operations report concurrency.map.read; Store,
Delete, and Clear report concurrency.map.write; Swap reports both.
LoadAndDelete, LoadOrStore, CompareAndSwap, and CompareAndDelete report both
with state_condition because mutation depends on the current mapping. Range also
retains callback uncertainty: its callback is not traversed and the iteration
is not a consistent snapshot. These categories identify API operations; the
read/write labels mean access/update intent, not formal Go memory-model
classification or a synchronization edge. They do not establish a shared
instance, successful mutation, observed synchronization, or correct concurrent
protocol. Local SSA stores are not reported as persistent/shared effects.
Exact sync.Pool.Get and Put calls report concurrency.pool.get and
concurrency.pool.put. Get may return an arbitrary pooled value, treat the pool
as empty, and invoke New; it retains callback uncertainty because pool contents
and New are not traced. Put may add a value that the runtime later removes
without notice. These identify API calls, not guaranteed item reuse, ownership
transfer, or state change. Go's memory-model relation applies only when Get
returns the same value passed to Put; the analyzer does not establish shared
pool identity or that relation for any pair of sites.
.PP
Exact crypto/x509.CertPool AddCert, AppendCertsFromPEM, and
AddCertWithConstraint calls report crypto.x509.certpool.add. This identifies an
attempted addition to an in-memory certificate pool, not proof the pool is used
for TLS trust, the certificate is valid or trusted, or the addition succeeded.
AppendCertsFromPEM retains argument_condition uncertainty because its input
controls whether any certificates are parsed and appended. AddCertWithConstraint
retains callback uncertainty: its constraint can run zero or more times later
during certificate verification and may run concurrently. Callback effects are
not inherited at registration. x509.NewCertPool is an effect-free boundary
because it creates an empty pool. Exact x509.SystemCertPool calls report
crypto.x509.certpool.system, identifying a request for a copy of the platform
system certificate pool. The call can return an error; environment overrides and
platform APIs can affect the source of roots. The effect does not establish
which roots were returned, call success, or later use for TLS verification, and
is not a general filesystem-read summary.
.PP
Resource acquisition/release is separate from filesystem mutation.
File.Close is resource.release, not fs.write. Network half-close is net.close
without full release.
HTTP/TLS shutdown
can retain callback uncertainty. These are possible operations, not successful
acquisitions, ownership, lifetime or leak proofs. File descriptors can refer to
pipes/devices/standard output. Constant os.OpenFile flags narrow effects;
unknown
flags retain possible reads, writes and creation.
.PP
io.Closer.Close, including embedded contracts such as io.ReadWriteCloser,
reports resource.release. Open implementation dispatch remains uncertain;
no concrete edge or network resource kind is inferred. Independently declared
application Close methods are not matched by name. Application persistence
interfaces retain API identity and reverse callers but are not classified as
filesystem/database effects based on names such as Save.
.PP
os.Rename and os.(*Root).Rename additionally report fs.rename, a distinct
category rather than an alias for fs.write. Existing fs.create/fs.remove entry
change categories remain for compatibility; they do not assert separate
create/remove calls or a new destination. This adds vocabulary without changing
JSON structures or source IDs. Use -effect rename or -api os.Rename.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
reffector -max-depth 0 ./billing
reffector -effect fs.write -max-depth 3 ./billing
reffector -api os.ReadFile -json ./internal
reffector -site 'site:<copied ID>' -max-depth 2 ./internal
reffector -unresolved -max-edges 100 ./internal
reffector -unresolved -reason interface_dispatch -operation ').Save' ./internal
reffector -all -json -effect http.response ./http
reffector -view functions -all -json ./billing
reffector -details -function '=Top' -max-paths 100 ./billing
.fi
.SH FUNCTION-VALUE PROVENANCE
Function-typed results of static calls into loaded non-standard bodies use
bounded symbolic return provenance, including parameter substitution through
helpers and tuple results. A closed non-nil singleton establishes the actual
invocation target. Finite alternatives use candidate_function_value edges and
possible_effects, not established effects or candidate_vta evidence. Even
common effects of summarized alternatives remain possible-only. Open flows
retain function_value uncertainty. Returning, storing or registering a callback
does not execute it; closure identity does not establish capture bindings.
.PP
Each query is bounded to 4096 work steps and 64 evaluator levels. Observed
function arguments at statically known calls can add candidate_function_value
edges at invoked formal parameters; caller completeness is not established, so
these calls remain open and candidate effects stay possible-only. For these
callback-argument candidates, dependency bodies contribute only within the
selected or budget-expanded VTA scope. Cycles,
exhaustion, external callback parameters, captured function loads, aliased
memory, struct/interface storage and opaque results remain open. No arbitrary
callback-helper specialization or heap analysis is performed. Go and defer
invocation forms and operation source sites are preserved.
.PP
Alternative summarized calls add optional possible_effects to site and operation
evidence, possible_sites to effect groups, and
possible_operation_sites_identified to coverage. Existing effects and group
sites remain established evidence. These additive fields are omitted when
unused. Effect filters include possible evidence; open possible-only calls are
also queryable as unresolved leads.

.SH LIMITATIONS
Follows static calls, closures, known local function values/phi merges,
generics,
goroutines, defers, and proven concrete interface receivers. Never expands open
interfaces to all loaded implementations. Missing bodies, cgo, assembly,
reflection, unknown stdlib APIs and unmodeled callbacks remain uncertain.
Branch feasibility, resource destinations/permissions, shared-memory mutation,
plugins, all platforms/tags and secondary driver effects are not modeled.
Lowered iterator callbacks and generated instructions without source locations
can lack complete provenance. Uncertainty does not enumerate all missing
effects.
.PP
Uses -mod=readonly and temporary workspace metadata so source module/workspace
checksums are not repaired. Go tooling may update its own caches. For offline
analysis set GOPROXY=off GOSUMDB=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local; missing dependencies are
an environment limit, not permission to download. This is not a tool sandbox.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0: analysis/help succeeded (including empty results). 1: loading/analysis/output
failed. 2: invalid flags.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR go (1),
.BR go-test (1),
.BR go-vet (1)
